.TH JTFRAME-MMR 1 "April 2026" "JTFRAME" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
jtframe-mmr \- generate memory-mapped register RTL
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jtframe mmr
.RI "[core-name] [flags]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBjtframe mmr\fR
reads
\fB$CORES/<core>/cfg/mmr.yaml\fR
and renders the template
\fB$JTFRAME/hdl/inc/mmr.v\fR
into generated Verilog modules under
\fB$CORES/<core>/hdl/\fR.
.PP
Each
\fBmmr.yaml\fR
entry defines one MMR block.
.SS Block Keys
.TP
.B include
Optional.
Imports definitions from another core.
It accepts:
\fBcore\fR
and
\fBname\fR.
\fBcore\fR
is mandatory and points to
\fB$CORES/<core>/cfg/mmr.yaml\fR.
\fBname\fR
can be omitted (imports all blocks),
one string (import one block), or a list of strings (import several blocks).
Examples:
\fB - include:\fR
\fB       core: shouse\fR
\fB       name: [cus30,cus116]\fR
\fB - include:\fR
\fB       core: shouse\fR
.TP
.B name
Required.
Block name.
The generated module is normally
\fBjt<core>_<name>_mmr.v\fR.
If
\fBno_core_name: true\fR
is set, the module becomes
\fBjt<name>_mmr.v\fR.
.TP
.B size
Required.
Number of bytes in the internal
\fBmmr[]\fR
array.
It must be at least 4.
.TP
.B dw
Optional.
Host bus width, either 8 or 16.
When
\fBdw: 16\fR
is used, the generated interface includes 16-bit
\fBdin\fR
and
\fBdout\fR
signals plus
\fBdsn\fR
byte strobes.
.TP
.B read_only
Optional.
When true, write handling and
\fBdout\fR
generation are omitted.
.TP
.B regs
Required.
List of exported register signals.
.SS Register Keys
.TP
.B name
Required.
Output signal name.
.TP
.B dw
Required unless
\fBevent\fR
is not
\fBnone\fR.
Output signal width.
.TP
.B at
Required.
Byte or bit mapping into
\fBmmr[]\fR.
Accepted forms are
\fBN\fR,
\fBN[B]\fR,
\fBN[MSB:LSB]\fR,
or a comma-separated list of those forms.
Example:
\fB0x10[7:0], 0x11[3:0]\fR.
.TP
.B event
Optional.
Select one-clock pulse generation for the first
\fBat\fR
address.
The default is
\fBnone\fR.
Accepted values are
\fBnone\fR,
\fBwrite\fR,
\fBany\fR,
\fBone\fR,
and
\fBzero\fR.
\fBwrite\fR
pulses on write access,
\fBany\fR
pulses on read or write access,
and
\fBone\fR
or
\fBzero\fR
pulse when writing that value to a bit-qualified
\fBat\fR
location.
If
\fBevent\fR
is not
\fBnone\fR
and
\fBdw\fR
is omitted or zero, the item is treated as event-only.
.TP
.B desc
Optional metadata only.
It does not affect code generation.
.SH PARAMETERS
The generated module carries three Verilog parameters. They are not set from
\fBmmr.yaml\fR; the instantiating code passes them.
.TP
.B SIMFILE
File the simulation restores the register file from. Defaults to
\fBrest.bin\fR, the block that
\fBdump2bin.sh\fR
leaves after every
\fBmem.yaml\fR
\fBioctl\fR
region.
.TP
.B SEEK
Byte offset of this block inside
\fBSIMFILE\fR.
Defaults to 0.
The generator does NOT assign these: with more than one MMR instance the
offsets are chosen by hand and must not overlap, and they must agree with
whatever produced the dump.
.TP
.B INIT
Power-on contents used in synthesis, packed
\fB{mmr[SIZE-1],...,mmr[0]}\fR.
Defaults to 0.
.SH SCENE REPLAY
This is what
\fBmmr.yaml\fR
is mainly for.
A scene replay simulation is built with
\fBNOMAIN\fR,
so there is no CPU to write the registers and every scroll register would read
zero.
The generated module reads
\fBSIMFILE\fR
at
\fBSEEK\fR
in an
\fBinitial\fR
block and loads it on reset, putting the chip back into the state it had when
the scene was captured.
Registers that the core never reads are still worth declaring, because whatever
is inside
\fBmmr[]\fR
is carried by the dump.
.PP
The scene can come from hardware, through the
\fBioctl_din\fR
port and a large enough
\fBJTFRAME_IOCTL_RD\fR,
or from a MAME script that writes the same byte layout.
Note that the hardware path only covers RAM declared in
\fBmem.yaml\fR
(at most six
\fBioctl\fR
regions); a core whose video RAM lives inside HDL chip models has to capture
from MAME.
.PP
Byte order:
\fBmmr[]\fR
is byte addressed, so on a 16-bit bus word N occupies bytes 2N (low) and 2N+1
(high) and the dump file is little endian, whatever the CPU is.
.SH FILES
The generated module lands in
\fB$CORES/<core>/hdl/\fR
next to hand written sources but is a build artefact:
\fB.gitignore\fR
carries
\fBcores/*/hdl/*_mmr.v\fR.
It still has to be listed in
\fBcfg/files.yaml\fR
like any other file, unless the core picks up
\fB*.v\fR
wholesale.
A hand written module named
\fB*_mmr.v\fR
is silently ignored by git; use
\fBgit add -f\fR
or another suffix.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show the short command help.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Enable verbose output.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jtframe (1),
.BR jtframe-parse (1),
.BR jtframe-mem (1)
